/Pg 11 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (This was led by a group of very large loans whose status moved to non-performing matured balloon or foreclosure. The five largest newly delinquent loans accounted for $2.6 billion of the $6.0 billion in newly delinquent balances, or roughly 44%. They included a showroom and exhibition-space portfolio split between North Carolina and Nevada, two Times Square properties in New York, a Chicago office tower, and a Seattle office portfolio.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (Non-performing matured balloon loans made up 66% of newly delinquent balances, 30-days-delinquent loans made up 23%, and loans in foreclosure made up 19%. The North Carolina and Nevada showroom portfolio, the Times Square mixed-use property, and the Seattle office portfolio transitioned from performing matured balloon to non-performing matured balloon. The Chicago office tower moved directly from current to non-performing matured balloon.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
103 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 79 0 R
/E (At the property-type level, four of the five major property types increased while one moved lower. Multifamily posted the largest increase, rising 46 basis points to 7.69% as a wave of Ohio, Texas, and New York multifamily loans became 30 days delinquent. Office increased 34 basis points to 11.91% as large loans on a Chicago office tower and a Seattle office portfolio became non-performing matured balloon and an Arlington, Virginia office tower became 30 days delinquent, outpacing the cure of two downtown Los Angeles )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
